  "rows": [
    {
      "source_id": "ega:III.7.7.12(ii)",
      "classification": "qualified_existing_derived",
      "counted_supported_unqualified": false,
      "hypotheses_and_scope": "Proper f:X->Y, locally Noetherian Y, homologically bounded-below complex P of coherent sheaves; no flatness over Y. Global hypertor is the fixed-base coefficient functor. The exchange conclusion uses scalar extension of this functor, not ordinary geometric pullback followed by recomputation over a new base ring.",
      "source_spans": [
        "fr:scalar-extension",
        "fr:flat-geometric-extension",
        "fr:exchange-theorem",
        "fr:nonflat-remark",
        "fr:cech-hypertor",
        "fr:flat-ce-resolution",
        "fr:sheafification-localization",
        "fr:coherent-hypertor",
        "fr:coefficient-map",
        "fr:fixed-flat-model",
        "fr:finite-free-replacement"
      ],
      "target_evidence": [
        "CECH",
        "RESOLVE",
        "LIFT",
        "KFLAT",
        "FREE",
        "NOETHERIAN",
        "BOUND",
        "COHERENT",
        "PROJECTION"
      ],
      "derivation": [
        "Work over Spec A in Y and set P^j=P_{-j}. Properness gives a finite affine cover with affine intersections. The alternating Cech direction has finite width, while P^j vanishes for j sufficiently large. The total C is therefore bounded above. CECH identifies it with RΓ(X,P). Its acyclicity hypotheses hold because the terms, cokernels and cohomology sheaves are quasi-coherent on affine opens.",
        "The historical III.6.6.2 construction takes tensor hyperhomology of Cech bicomplexes. For the second factor (id_S,M), its Cech complex is just M[0]. By III.6.6.6 one can use a projective Cartan-Eilenberg resolution of the first bicomplex only. Its total E is bounded above projective: after homological reindexing all three indices have lower bounds, so every total degree has finitely many summands. E represents C and is K-flat. RESOLVE and LIFT compare it, up to homotopy, with a modern bounded-above projective resolution. Tensoring the comparison with arbitrary M preserves it. Thus global hypertor is H^{-p}(C tensor_A^L M), naturally including coefficient connecting maps.",
        "Localization is the same in both constructions: finite Cech totals and projective resolutions commute with flat localization. This is exactly the localization used to glue the source in III.6.7.1-6.7.2. Consequently the sheaf identity is T_p(M)=H^{-p}(Rf_*P tensor_Y^L M). PROJECTION also identifies it with H^{-p} Rf_*(P tensor_X^L Lf^*M). No ordinary nonflat tensor is substituted.",
        "C is bounded above and has finite cohomology. For a fixed degree q choose a sufficiently low cutoff a. BOUND makes RΓ(tau_{<a}P) vanish in degrees q and q+1. Hence H^q(C)=H^q RΓ(tau_{>=a}P). The latter truncation is bounded coherent, so COHERENT applies. NOETHERIAN and FREE now give a single bounded-above finite-free complex L representing C. L tensor M computes all T_p(M), and its finite rank terms, not perfection of C, are sufficient.",
        "For A->B and a B-module M', keep the original base A and define the extension by III.7.1.3: T_p^(B)(M')=H^{-p}(L tensor_A M')=H^{-p}((L tensor_A B) tensor_B M'). This is also the n=1 case of III.6.10.1 with an additional identity factor over Spec B. Its canonical exchange is H^{-p}(L) tensor_A B -> H^{-p}(L tensor_A B). With the first Cech factor fixed, the source map in III.6.7.10 is induced by the same coefficient map; III.6.10.1.2 records precisely this compatibility.",
        "The previously checked III.7.7.5 argument now applies to L: upper semicontinuity of residue dimensions, right-exactness/tensor exchange, adjacent left-exactness/Hom representation, and affine locality. The equivalence with exchange for every A-algebra follows in reverse by taking the square-zero algebra A direct-sum M and its M summand. Local identifications glue. This uses the coefficient-extension convention, which must be stated explicitly after removing flatness.",
        "The competing ordinary-geometric reading is false. Take A=k[t], Y=X=Spec A, f=id, and P=A/(t) in degree zero. The finite-free model is A --t--> A in cohomological degrees -1,0. T_1(A)=0 and T_1(k)=k, so T_1 is not right exact (test A->k). Correct coefficient extension to B=k gives exchange 0->k. If instead one pulls P back ordinarily and recomputes over each B, the identity morphism gives Tor_1^B(P tensor_A B,B)=0 for every B; the naive exchange maps are all 0->0. That would incorrectly satisfy condition (d) while right exactness fails. This is a test of an interpretation, not a new Stacks or historical-source erratum."
      ],
      "resolved_residuals": [
        "Historical global-hypertor totalization identified with the modern derived coefficient functor in the actual bounded-below homological domain.",
        "Canonical fixed-base coefficient exchange map identified, including nonflat A->B.",
        "Single bounded-above finite-free representative and the derived coefficient version of the III.7.7.5 conclusions established."
      ],
      "remaining_qualification": "Do not read the extension as ordinary geometric pullback of P followed by recomputation of hypertor over Y'. That reading is refuted. Original printed-page collation is not claimed; no source correction or registry admission is made.",
      "not_a_missing_stacks_theorem": true
    }
  ],
